fetchconfig -- README / Documentation
=====================================

A Perl script for retrieving and backing up the configuration of network devices
at scale  -  over telnet, SSH or a device's web UI, across dozens of vendors,
with change tracking, e-mail summaries, and a repository you can script against.

Introduction
============

fetchconfig is a Perl script for retrieving the configuration of multiple
devices. It has been tested under Linux, AIX and Windows (on native Windows:
telnet, web-UI and SNMP devices only, no SSH  -  see Platform support), and
currently supports a variety of devices, including Cisco IOS, Cisco Catalyst,
Cisco ASA, Cisco PIX, FortiGate Firewalls, ProCurve, Alcatel Ethernet Routers
(aka Riverstone), Dell PowerConnect Switches, Terayon 3200/3500 CMTS, Datacom
DmSwitch Switches, HP MSR Routers, Mikrotik Routers, Tellabs MSR Routers,
Juniper EX Switch JunOS, Oracle Acme Packet, Audiocodes Mediant, Cisco IOS XR,
NEC Univerge IX, Hirschmann Railswitch, Zyxel switches, PLANET managed switches,
Aruba CX switches, Cisco Nexus switches, AudioCodes Mediant SBCs, TP-Link Easy
Smart switches, HP ProCurve 1700 series switches, and HP ProCurve ProVision
(incl. 4000M/8000M) over SNMP+TFTP.

Key concepts. A device table lists the devices to back up, one per line, each
naming a model (how to talk to that kind of device) and options. fetchconfig
connects to each device, retrieves its configuration, and stores it in a
repository (a dated directory tree), keeping a configurable history. Reading on:
Device table defines the input, Template model covers the flexible generic
model, and Options reference lists every command-line option.

    NOTE: Companion web UI
    Need a browser-based UI? A companion project, fetchconfig-web, provides a
    web front-end on top of fetchconfig for browsing backups, viewing and
    comparing configurations, checking run status, and editing the device table:
    <https://github.com/rainer-tammer/fetchconfig-web>

    NOTE: For developers
    DEVELOPER-GUIDE.md / .html is the architecture and engineering guide
    (internals, data structures, device-table format, security model, and how to
    extend); How-To-add-a-model.md / .html is the programming manual for writing
    a new device model. perl Makefile.PL && make test (or prove -I. t/) runs the
    test suite under t/, and make doc regenerates the .md-derived docs via
    tools/render-doc.py and tools/md2html.py (launched through tools/pyrun,
    which selects python3 on Linux and python on AIX). Perl module requirements
    (core vs CPAN for Perl 5.26/5.28/5.40, by feature) are in the file
    DEPENDENCIES.

