Upgrading & compatibility
=========================

This release is the 9.x line of fetchconfig, a continuation of upstream
fetchconfig 0.25.

Config- and command-line-compatible with 0.25. A device table and the command
lines you used with upstream 0.25 keep working unchanged  -  the 9.x line is a
drop-in replacement, and no existing configuration has to be rewritten to
upgrade. New behaviour is added through new options and new models, never by
changing the meaning of what 0.25 already understood. CHANGES.md (entry 9.56)
summarises how the 9.x fork differs from 0.25  -  new models and transports,
backup-file and comparison improvements, and the command-line additions below.

A few changes alter behaviour on first upgrade, even though the configuration
itself still parses. Read these before the first run of a new version on an
existing repository:

    WARNING: v9.53  -  trailing newline (affects every device)
    Saved configuration files now end with a trailing newline on the last line,
    matching the device's own output (and a config downloaded from a device web
    UI). This touches every model. Because the file content changes by that one
    final byte, the first backup run after upgrading reports every device as
    "changed" and, with changes_only, saves a fresh copy  -  a one-time effect
    of the fix, not real drift. The second run onward compares clean.

    WARNING: v9.55  -  the backup filename suffix must start with "."
    Starting with v9.55, filename_append_suffix must start with a "." (e.g.
    .txt). A device whose suffix lacks the leading dot, or contains / or
    whitespace, now fails its backup with an error and writes nothing. Fix any
    dotless suffix in your device table before the first v9.55 run. Also: do not
    add, remove or change a suffix on a device that already has backups; use
    fetchconfig.pl -S to find devices whose existing backups already mix
    suffixes.

    NOTE: v9.65  -  generic-model template grammar (generic model only)
    This affects only the template-driven generic model, which postdates 0.25  -
    a 0.25 device table has no templates to migrate. Since v9.65 every template
    state is declared with the state keyword, including the per-transport entry
    states, so a template written for v9.64 or earlier no longer parses. To
    migrate a template, add state before each bare entry-state header (e.g.
    state_login_ssh -> state state_login_ssh). The shipped templates and
    tools/generate_template.pl were updated accordingly; see
    README.generic-model.

Installation
============

1. fetchconfig.pl expects a Perl interpreter at /usr/bin/perl.
2. Install the Perl modules you need  -  see Perl module requirements. Most are
   in the Perl core (5.26+); the non-core ones are Net::Telnet and Net::SMTP
   (every run), Net::OpenSSH (SSH devices), LWP::UserAgent/HTTP::Request::Common
   (web-UI models), and Net::SNMP/Net::TFTP (procurve-snmp).
3. Unpack the fetchconfig tarball at a suitable location.

    # fetchconfig tarball available at /tmp/fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz
    cd /usr/local
    tar xzf /tmp/fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz
    ln -s fetchconfig-0.0 fetchconfig
    # fetchconfig is now available under /usr/local/fetchconfig

1. fetchconfig.pl expects to find a Perl interpreter in the PATH.
2. Install the Perl modules you need  -  see Perl module requirements. Most are
   in the Perl core (5.26+); the non-core ones are Net::Telnet and Net::SMTP
   (every run), Net::OpenSSH (SSH devices), LWP::UserAgent/HTTP::Request::Common
   (web-UI models), and Net::SNMP/Net::TFTP (procurve-snmp). On native Windows
   the SSH models do not run (Net::OpenSSH/IO::Pty are unavailable there), so
   only telnet, web-UI and procurve-snmp devices work; backup comparison (-g ...
   -m) and change-report diffs are disabled, -P always runs sequentially, and
   chmod/umask hardening is a no-op (rely on folder ACLs). Under Cygwin/WSL it
   behaves as on Linux. See Platform support.
3. Unpack the fetchconfig tarball at a suitable location. From the command line,
   gzip (http://www.gzip.org) and tar
   (http://gnuwin32.sourceforge.net/packages/tar.htm) can be used  -  or a GUI
   archiver such as WinZip.

    :: fetchconfig tarball available at c:\temp\fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz
    c:
    cd \
    gzip -cd c:\temp\fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz | tar xvf -
    move fetchconfig-0.0 fetchconfig
    :: fetchconfig is now available under c:\fetchconfig

    NOTE: Tarball name is a placeholder
    fetchconfig-0.0.tar.gz (and the unpacked fetchconfig-0.0 directory) above
    are placeholders  -  replace them with the actual name of the release you
    downloaded. The name varies by how you obtained it: a GitHub release or tag
    download, for example, is named for the tag or commit rather than 0.0.

Platform support
================

fetchconfig runs on Linux, AIX and Windows. On Linux and AIX all features are
available. On native Windows (Strawberry/ActivePerl) the features below rely on
POSIX facilities and are unavailable; everything else works normally.

- SSH models are not supported. Net::OpenSSH and IO::Pty do not run on native
  Windows, so the *-ssh models and the generic model's transport=ssh cannot
  connect. Telnet, web-UI and procurve-snmp devices work normally.
- Config diffs are not produced (they need an external diff). The -g ... -m
  backup comparison exits with an error. The change report and summary e-mail
  are still generated, but a changed device is shown as diff-unavailable instead
  of a diff; email_max_diff does not switch this off (it only caps how many
  diffs are embedded when diffs are available).
- -P always runs sequentially. There is no fork, so -P N>1 is accepted but
  reduced to -P 1.
- File-permission hardening is a no-op. chmod 0600 on .debug files and the umask
  on lock/backup files have no effect on Windows; confidentiality relies on the
  containing folder's NTFS ACLs.

    NOTE: Cygwin / WSL
    Under Cygwin or WSL, fetchconfig behaves as on Linux  -  SSH, diffs and
    parallel fetch all work.

Perl module requirements
========================

fetchconfig runs on Perl 5.26 or later (tested on 5.26, 5.28 and 5.40; see the
DEPENDENCIES file for the full module list and core-vs-CPAN status). Most of the
modules it uses are in the Perl core on those versions and need no separate
installation (Text::ParseWords, POSIX, IO::Handle, Time::HiRes, MIME::Base64,
FindBin, File::Temp, File::Path, File::Basename, File::Compare, Getopt::Long,
Digest::SHA). The modules below are not in the core and must be installed
separately  -  but only if you use the devices that need them.

Module(s)                              Needed for
-------------------------------------  -----------------------------------------
Net::Telnet                            Every run. Telnet devices, and also
                                       driving the pseudo-terminal for all SSH
                                       devices - in practice fetchconfig cannot
                                       back up any device without it.
Net::SMTP                              Every run. Part of the core libnet
                                       distribution (core since Perl 5.7.3), so
                                       no separate installation is needed.
                                       fetchconfig loads its reporting code on
                                       every run - it writes the per-device
                                       status file even when no e-mail is
                                       configured - so this must be present
                                       regardless. No SMTP connection is made
                                       unless an email: line is configured.
Net::OpenSSH                           All SSH-based models: cisco-ios-ssh,
                                       cisco-asa-ssh, comware-ssh, procurve-ssh,
                                       planet-ssh, aruba-cx-ssh and any other
                                       *-ssh model.
LWP::UserAgent, HTTP::Request::Common  The web-UI models tplink-web-sg105e and
                                       procurve-web-1700 (no CLI). Both come
                                       with the libwww-perl distribution.
Net::SNMP, Net::TFTP                   The procurve-snmp model (HP ProCurve
                                       ProVision, including the 4000M/8000M
                                       chassis), which backs up over SNMP +
                                       TFTP.
IO::Socket::SSL (needs Net::SSLeay)    Only if TLS is enabled for the summary
                                       e-mail (email: tls=starttls or tls=ssl).
                                       Plain SMTP does not need it. Mozilla::CA
                                       is optional, for a CA bundle where the
                                       system has none.

    # install the non-core modules you need, e.g.
    cpan install Net::Telnet Net::OpenSSH
    cpan install LWP::UserAgent Net::SNMP Net::TFTP

Usage
=====

1. Edit device_table.example according to your needs, and save it as
   device_table.

    # fetchconfig available under /usr/local/fetchconfig
    cd /usr/local/fetchconfig
    cp device_table.example device_table
    vi device_table

2. Run fetchconfig.pl:

    cd /usr/local/fetchconfig
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table

fetchconfig.pl scans the device_table file, tries to retrieve the configuration
for every device, and stores the configuration files under the repository.

A relative -devices= name is looked for in the current directory first, then
beside fetchconfig.pl (its own directory), so a device table kept with the
script is found from any working directory  -  a cron job, say  -  without a cd
first. An absolute path is used exactly as given.

By default the devices are fetched one after another, in device-table order.
With many devices the run time is dominated by the network sessions; -P N
fetches N devices concurrently  -  see Parallel fetching. On native Windows -P N
always runs sequentially (no fork; see Platform support).

Device table
============

The device table assigned with -devices= is a text file that supports two types
of lines.

1. Default-options lines
------------------------

Assign default options to a device model. The following example adds default
options to the cisco-ios model:

    #default	model		model-default-options
    #
    default:        cisco-ios       user=backup,pass=fran,enable=jose

2. Device lines
---------------

Define attributes of every device, and optionally assign device-specific
options. The following example defines a device named vpn-gw:

    #model		dev-unique-id	device-host	[device-options]
    #
    cisco-ios       vpn-gw          192.168.0.1     keep=10,changes_only=1

    NOTE: dev-unique-id constraints
    Must be (a) an unique identifier across all devices, and (b) a valid
    filesystem's directory name.

Beside the two line types above, the device table also accepts the control
directives email: (see E-mail notifications), db: (see Backup-history database)
and directory: (see Directory allowlist).

Device table on the command line
================================

Valid lines for the device table file can also be passed directly through the
-line=string switch.

    /path/to/fetchconfig.pl -line="default: tellabs-msr user=backup,pass=s&cr&t,timeout=10,keep=5,changes_only=0,fetch_timeout=1200,filename_append_suffix=.txt,repository=/path/to/repo" -line="tellabs-msr lab-router 10.0.0.1"

Directory allowlist
===================

Restrict which directories a repository=, template_dir=, on_fetch_run=, report
or lock directory may name.

Optionally, the device table may restrict which directories a repository= or
template_dir= option is allowed to name. This is useful when the device table is
edited through a less-trusted front end (e.g. a web UI): the allowlist lives
only in the real config file and cannot be widened from the UI, so an edit can
never point a device at an arbitrary path.

With all four directory types allowed:

    directory: repository $REPO1     /usr/local/fetchconfig/config
    directory: template   $TEMPLATE1 /usr/local/fetchconfig/templates
    directory: fetch_run  $CMD1      /var/fetchconfig/fetch_run
    directory: report    $REP1       /var/www/fetchconfig/reports

With on_fetch_run disabled (fetch_run none); a section must still cover all
four types, so report is present:

    directory: repository $REPO1     /usr/local/fetchconfig/config
    directory: template   $TEMPLATE1 /usr/local/fetchconfig/templates
    directory: fetch_run  none
    directory: report     $REP1      /var/www/fetchconfig/reports

Each line is directory: <type> <$TAG> <path>, where type is repository,
template, fetch_run, report or lock, $TAG is a unique $NAME symbol, and path is
the allowed directory. A section must cover all four types; fetch_run constrains
on_fetch_run= (the directory of the command's program must be an allowed
fetch_run directory), and directory: fetch_run none forbids on_fetch_run
entirely. Likewise directory: report none satisfies the report type and disables
change-report writing entirely.

Enforcement is opt-in: with no directory: line, nothing is restricted beyond the
system-directory rule below (existing device tables are unaffected). The
system-directory rule below always applies: a system directory is rejected
whether or not an allowlist is present. Since v9.68 each distinct non-system
repository=, template_dir= and on_fetch_run directory in use is then reported
once per run as warning: ... has no directory: allowlist entry (unrestricted
...), so you can see which paths are unrestricted and decide whether to add a
directory: section (a system directory never reaches this warning  -  it is
rejected first). With at least one present, every repository= and template_dir=
used by a device (or inherited from a default: line) must be allowed:

- repository= must name a repository entry; template_dir= a template entry;
  on_fetch_run= must run a program in a fetch_run entry (types are not
  interchangeable).
- The value may be the $TAG or the literal path; a $TAG is expanded internally.
- Matching is exact, ignoring a single trailing slash. Paths are not
  canonicalized, so a symlinked, version-free path (e.g. /usr/local/fetchconfig
  -> /usr/local/fetchconfig-9.64) is compared as written and the version stays
  hidden.
- Every path is checked for illegal content: no /../ or /./, must be absolute,
  and only characters valid for the host OS.
- System directories are never accepted, allowlist or not (since v9.68): /bin,
  /sbin, /lib, /lib64, /usr/bin, /usr/sbin, /usr/lib, /usr/lib64, /usr/share,
  /usr/include, /etc, /boot, /dev, /proc, /sys, /run, /var/run, /var/lock, the
  root /, and anything under them. A repository=, template_dir= or on_fetch_run
  program there is rejected and the device skipped.

A value off the list, of the wrong type, an undefined tag, or with illegal
content is rejected at fetch time with a clear error and that device is skipped;
a bad value inherited from a default: line rejects every device that inherits
it. --list-allowed-dirs prints the parsed allowlist.

E-mail notifications
====================

fetchconfig.pl can e-mail a summary of a run's backup results as an HTML table
(Device-ID, Date, Time, State, Size, Changes). This is entirely optional: unless
an email: line is present, no SMTP connection is ever attempted.

The message is laid out as a white rounded card on a grey canvas, with a
dark-blue (#1f4e79) header bar carrying a small logo and "fetchconfig vX.Y", and
the copyright footer below the card  -  matching the fetchconfig-web mails. The
header logo is embedded in the message as a multipart/related inline image
(Content-ID <fcwlogo>); it is independent of the report_logo= used inside the
on-disk HTML report, which is unchanged. The message is multipart: the HTML part
above, and a plain-text part carrying the same statistics and per-device tables
rendered as plain text (auto-sized columns, no diffs), so a text-only mail
client or a notification preview still shows the result summary.

1. Configure the sender, recipient, and SMTP server
---------------------------------------------------

Add an email: line to the default options section (it is independent of any
device model):

    #email:	options
    #
    email:	from=config.backup@acme.com,to=admin@acme.com,smtp=mail.acme.com

Key       Requirement  Description
--------  -----------  ---------------------------------------------------------
from      Mandatory    The envelope/header sender address.
to        Mandatory    Recipient who receives the status of every device backed
                       up in the run. Multiple addresses are separated by ; (not
                       a comma, since commas already separate options).
smtp      Mandatory    SMTP server, as host or host:port (default port 25).
                       Plain SMTP only - no encryption.
user      Optional     SMTP AUTH LOGIN username.
password  Optional     SMTP AUTH LOGIN password. Only used (and AUTH attempted)
                       when user is also given.
footer    Optional     Copyright/attribution text shown in the summary e-mail's
                       footer. The - Version <n> that follows it is fixed and
                       always appended. If the value contains a comma, wrap it
                       in double quotes (the quotes are stripped). Default: (c)
                       2006-2026 Everton da Silva Marques, Rainer Tammer and
                       others.
port      Optional     TCP port of the SMTP server; works with every tls
                       setting. Precedence: an explicit port= beats a port given
                       as part of the host (smtp=mail.acme.com:587), which beats
                       the default. The default follows the TLS mode - 25 for
                       off/starttls, 465 for ssl - so tls=ssl works without also
                       setting the port. Must be 1-65535.
tls       Optional     Transport security for the SMTP connection: off (default,
                       plain SMTP), starttls (connect in clear, upgrade with
                       STARTTLS; typically port 25/587) or ssl (TLS from the
                       start; typically port 465). Both TLS modes need
                       IO::Socket::SSL (and Net::SSLeay); plain SMTP needs
                       neither. If TLS is requested but unavailable, or the
                       handshake fails, the mail is not sent - fetchconfig never
                       falls back to plaintext. AUTH LOGIN sends the password
                       base64-encoded (not encrypted), so use tls whenever the
                       server is not on a trusted network.

email: may be repeated; later values overwrite earlier ones for the same key
(same rule as default:). A value may be wrapped in double quotes to include a
comma (e.g. footer="(c) 2026 ACME, Inc."); the quotes are stripped.

2. Override the recipient per device
------------------------------------

    #model		dev-unique-id	hostname	device-options
    #
    parks		lab209r		10.30.0.6	to=lab-owner@acme.com

A device whose recipient was overridden this way is left out of no one's mail:
it is bundled with every other device sharing that same override recipient into
one separate e-mail, containing just their own device(s). The globally
configured recipient still receives the status of every device processed in the
run, including overridden ones.

3. The "Changes" column
-----------------------

Only reflects a changed-vs-previous comparison for devices where the run already
performs one (changes_only=1, or on_fetch_run/on_fetch_cat set); for other
devices it is reported as n/a  -  no extra repository scan is performed just for
this report.

Change reports
==============

An HTML report of what changed, with coloured diffs; summary in e-mail, full
report via fetchconfig-web.

On native Windows the per-device diffs are disabled (they need an external
diff); changed devices still appear in the report, marked "diff unavailable".
See Platform support.

After a run fetchconfig can write a self-contained HTML change report (summary
table plus a coloured diff per changed device) to an allowlisted report
directory, and embed up to email_max_diff device diffs in the summary e-mail
with a link to the full report. The report directory is presented by
fetchconfig-web (view/download/delete) and is not meant to be served to users
directly. Options on email: lines:

- report_dir=$REP1  -  the report directory (a report allowlist entry).
- write_report=on|off  -  write the report. With on and no from=/to=/smtp=, only
  the report is written (no e-mail).
- email_max_diff=N  -  embed up to N changed devices' diffs in the e-mail
  (device-table order). Default 0 (no diffs); 0 or unset = none.
- web_report_url=URL  -  base URL of fetchconfig-web (the CGI itself, no query
  string, e.g. https://host/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi); fetchconfig appends
  ?action=view_report&file=<report> automatically for the e-mail's full-report
  link (emitted only when a report was written; any query string already on the
  value is ignored).
- report_logo=FILE|none  -  a logo in report_dir, embedded (self-contained),
  recommended 250x50 px.
- report_days=N  -  prune reports older than N days (only if N>1), by exact
  filename.
- mask_secrets=on|off  -  mask common secret forms in diffs (default on); master
  switch for all hiding.
- report_hide=<regex>  -  additional custom regex (one per line, accumulates);
  the matched substring becomes ****. Governed by mask_secrets.

Device states in the report: initial (first backup, no diff), Changed (diff
shown), Unchanged, Failed, and diff-unavailable (a changed device whose previous
backup could not be read).

Secret masking is best effort  -  it masks common Cisco/ProCurve forms after
diffing, on both old and new lines (a rotated secret shows as **** -> ****), but
is vendor-incomplete. The built-in forms include SNMP community, type-5/7
secrets, enable secret hashes, key-string, pre-shared-key, wpa-psk,
wpa-passphrase, a bare key <hex> and the Hirschmann passwd <user> :vN:<hash>:
form (username kept, hash masked). Reports and report e-mails must be treated as
sensitive; the stored configs in the repository still contain cleartext
credentials regardless of report masking  -  the report only masks what it
displays, not what is stored.

Custom patterns. For secrets the built-ins miss, add regexes with report_hide,
one per line  -  only the substring each regex matches becomes ****, so match
just the value (a fixed-width lookbehind keeps the keyword):

    email: report_hide=(?<=wpa-passphrase )\S+
    email: report_hide=(?<=^key )[0-9a-f]{16,}

turning wpa-passphrase c1ca253b... into wpa-passphrase ****. Take care not to
over-match (e.g. (?<=secret )\S+ also fires inside my-secret). Patterns
accumulate; comment a line out to disable it. They are governed by mask_secrets
(with mask_secrets=off nothing is hidden), and an invalid regex is logged and
skipped. For report_hide the whole rest of the line is taken verbatim as the
regex  -  no comma splitting, no quote stripping, no space trimming  -  so a
regex may contain commas (e.g. {16,}), = and spaces with no escaping. Do not
wrap it in quotes (they would become part of the pattern). One regex per line.

Each device's diff is wrapped in an armored <div class="device-diff"
data-device="<id>" id="dev-<id>"> with a stable structure, so fetchconfig-web
can parse it into an expandable per-device view. Treat this markup as a parse
contract.

Report directory setup. So that fetchconfig-web (running as the web server's
user/group) can read and manage the reports, set the report directory's group to
the web server's group and set the setgid bit, e.g. chgrp <webgroup>
/var/www/fetchconfig/reports and chmod g+s /var/www/fetchconfig/reports. With
g+s, each report file inherits the directory's group automatically  -
fetchconfig needs no special privileges and does not change ownership itself.

Backup-history database (optional)
==================================

Record each run's backup health and config-change history to a PostgreSQL
database, for a fetchconfig-web dashboard that scales to thousands of devices
without walking the repository on every page. OFF by default and entirely
optional: with no db: line fetchconfig stays file-based and DBI/DBD::Pg are
never loaded. The per-device .status file is unchanged; the database is a
separate, additional sink.

The schema and the writer login role are created and owned by fetchconfig-web
(run fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl --upgrade, which creates the login role and
grants it the required SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE). The engine only writes rows: it
never issues DDL and never creates roles. PostgreSQL 8.2 or newer is supported
(no ON CONFLICT/MERGE/jsonb is used).

1. Enable it
------------

Add a single db: line to the default options section. It is fleet-wide, not a
per-device option:

    #db:	key=value,...
    db:	db_inst=fetchconfig,db_user=fetchconfig_dashboard,db_pass=secret,db_host=db.acme.com

Key                 Requirement  Description
------------------  -----------  -----------------------------------------------
db_inst             Mandatory    Database (instance) name.
db_user             Mandatory    Writer login role. No default is applied;
                                 fetchconfig_dashboard is only the name
                                 fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl creates by
                                 convention.
db_pass             Optional     Its password. Normal device_table quoting, so a
                                 value containing a comma or equals sign must be
                                 double-quoted (db_pass="a,b=c"). Never written
                                 to the log.
db_host             Mandatory    host or host:port. IPv6 uses brackets to carry
                                 a port, [2001:db8::1]:5432; a bare 2001:db8::1
                                 is the host at the default port (5432).
db_connect_timeout  Optional     Seconds to wait for the connection (default 5).
                                 Kept short so a dead database can never stall a
                                 run.
db_sslmode          Optional     libpq sslmode: disable, require or verify-full.
                                 Default disable (the DB is normally local on
                                 the same box). Use require or stronger when the
                                 database is across a WAN.
db_max_outbox_days  Optional     Spool (outbox) retention in days (default 90).
                                 See below.

db: may be repeated; later values overwrite earlier ones for the same key (same
rule as default: and email:). The device_table holds db_pass, so it must stay
mode 0600 (see Security notes).

2. How it writes (store-and-forward)
------------------------------------

Nothing blocks the run, and an unreachable database never costs a backup:

- During the run nothing touches the database. Each run appends its records to a
  per-repository write-ahead spool file, <repository>/.db-<pid>-<timestamp>.
- At the end of the run, in the parent and whether or not email: is configured,
  the spool files are drained into the database one transaction per file, oldest
  first. A file is deleted only after its transaction commits.
- If the database is unreachable the run still completes normally; the spool
  files accumulate and are drained on a later run. Spool files older than
  db_max_outbox_days are dropped (after that long an outage, the on-disk backups
  and the .status files remain the source of truth).

All writes use DBI placeholders; the append-only tables use INSERT ... WHERE NOT
EXISTS and the per-device current row is UPDATE-else-INSERT, so replaying a
spool file is idempotent.

3. What it records
------------------

Three tables, all owned by fetchconfig-web: a dense backup_runs (one row per
device per run), a sparse backup_versions (one row per retained backup, carrying
its sha1), and backup_current (one row per device: its latest state and
last-good backup). All timestamps are stored as the box's local wall-clock time,
so every column is consistent on a single-host install; fetchconfig-web renders
them as-is, with no timezone conversion.

Every run (with or without email:) logs a -----[DB writer]----- section with the
DB-history result: all N record(s) written; outbox empty, or INCOMPLETE - M
run(s) still pending (oldest <ts>); will retry next run when the database was
unreachable and the records were spooled (connect/drain warnings appear in the
same section). Nothing is logged when no db: is set. When email: is configured,
the summary mail shows the result as a status box inside the card: green when
everything was written, red when records are still buffered for a later run, and
grey (history export not configured) when no db: is set.

Backfilling existing backups. When you first enable db:, only new runs populate
the database  -  the backups already on disk are not in it. Run --db-backfill
once to reconstruct the history from those files: one backup_versions row (its
sha1 recomputed from the file, identical to a live save) and one backup_runs row
per stored backup, plus a backup_current row for each device not yet recorded.
It is idempotent and repeatable, never overwrites a row a live run wrote, and -T
previews the counts. Only saved backups can be rebuilt  -  the dense run history
(unchanged or failed runs leave no file) accrues from live runs afterwards.

Per-version line deltas. Each backup_versions row can carry lines_added and
lines_removed  -  the +added/-removed of the ignore-normalized diff against the
device's previous version, so a same-length edit is not reported as a zero-byte
change. The two columns belong to the fetchconfig-web 2.12 schema; the engine
checks once per run whether they exist and writes them only then, so an older
web side keeps working with the columns unpopulated. The live writer computes
the delta over the same normalized view it uses for change detection.
--db-backfill also records them for existing backups and UPDATEs rows written
earlier whose deltas are still empty; a backfilled generic-template device
counts raw lines (its template is not loaded during a backfill), whereas live
runs count the normalized view.

Device support
==============

Known to be supported  -  not a comprehensive list. Other devices and versions
are likely to work but haven't been verified.

    NOTE: Windows
    The SSH models (cisco-ios-ssh, cisco-asa-ssh, comware-ssh, procurve-ssh,
    planet-ssh, aruba-cx-ssh, nexus-ssh) and the SSH transport of mediant-sbc
    are not available on native Windows (Net::OpenSSH/IO::Pty do not run there);
    their telnet counterparts, and mediant-sbc over telnet, work. See Platform
    support.

