Need a device that isn't listed? Two routes: describe it with a
 template (see Template-driven model below),
 or write a per-device Perl model  -  see
 How to add a model (How-To-add-a-model.html).

Template-driven model (generic)
===============================

Describe a device with a template instead of a Perl module. On native Windows
the template model's transport=ssh is unavailable (SSH needs
Net::OpenSSH/IO::Pty); use transport=telnet. See Platform support.

Besides the per-device Perl models, fetchconfig has a generic model whose device
interaction is described by a template file rather than Perl code. A template is
a small state machine of expect/send steps, with the transport, prompt,
timeouts, interrupt screens and ignore-lines as data. For a clean-CLI device
this is a short file rather than a ~200-line module, and similar devices can
share or clone one template.

A device selects it in the device table with the model generic and a template
name:

    generic  sw1  10.0.0.1  model=cisco-ios,transport=ssh,user=...

This loads templates/cisco-ios.tmpl; transport is ssh or telnet. Templates for
Cisco IOS/IOS-XE (both transports) and HP ProCurve (menu/VT100) ship as worked
examples. Two companion tools support the workflow: tools/record-session.pl
captures a real session as a byte-accurate log to build a template from, and
tools/generate_template.pl scaffolds a template draft from such a capture. For
Windows users without Perl, contrib/windows/ contains Python ports of these two
tools (record_session.py, generate_template.py) with the same command line and
file formats; build.cmd packages them into standalone .exes with PyInstaller.
See contrib/windows/README.windows.

A template can be checked without a device:

    ./fetchconfig.pl --check-template templates/cisco-ios.tmpl

The argument must be the full path to the .tmpl file  -  a bare template name is
not resolved, because the same name can exist in different template_dir
directories. This runs the grammar and the load-time validator (self-loops, goto
targets, capture markers, reachability, transport entry) and prints ok or the
errors; it exits 0 ok, 1 errors, 2 file problem, and needs no device table. It
is a structural check only  -  a template that passes may still need adjusting
against the real device.

To list the templates available to the loaded devices:

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -t

-t (--list-template) scans every template_dir= a loaded device configures, plus
the default template directory, and prints the template names it finds,
numbered, one per line. Exit 0 if any template is found, 1 if none.

The generic model's device-table options  -  and the template-only directives  -
are listed under Per-model option tables in the Options reference.

    Further reading
    The generic model and its tools are documented separately:

    - README.generic-model (README.generic-model.html)  -  the generic model &
    template engine: architecture, template grammar, execution semantics,
    writing and generating templates, and the record-session.pl capture tool
    - How to add a model (How-To-add-a-model.html)  -  writing a new per-device
    Perl model (the alternative to a template)

The template parser is production-ready; the execution engine has been verified
on Cisco IOS-XE over telnet and SSH and on an HP ProCurve menu switch. Verify a
template against the target device before relying on it.

Parallel fetching
=================

Fetching is sequential unless asked otherwise: one device at a time, in
device-table order, every log line shown as it happens. With thousands of
devices the run time is dominated by the network sessions (roughly 5-15 s each,
plus a full timeout for every unreachable device), and a sequential run no
longer fits in a night.

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -P 20

-P N fetches with N concurrent worker processes. The parent loads the device
table, then hands out one device at a time to whichever worker asks next, so a
slow device never holds up the others. Every worker is a copy of the fully
loaded parent, so the models run unchanged. With N = 10-20 a run of several
hours typically completes in well under one; the gain is bounded by what the
devices and the network tolerate, not by the backup host.

    NOTE: Output is atomic per device
    While a worker fetches, everything it writes to stdout and stderr is
    buffered; when the device finishes, the parent copies that buffer to the
    real streams in one uninterrupted block. One device's output is therefore
    never mixed with another's  -  each block looks exactly as in a sequential
    run. Two consequences: blocks appear in completion order, not table order;
    and a device's lines appear when it finishes, not live. At -P 1 nothing is
    buffered and behaviour is unchanged.

Everything else is unaffected: each device writes only its own directory,
.status and .debug; the summary e-mail, per-device to= bundling and the .status
files are produced by the parent from the workers' results, exactly as in a
sequential run; keep=N expiry and changes_only comparison run per device as
before. A worker that dies is detected by the parent; the device it was fetching
is recorded as failed and the remaining devices are still fetched by the other
workers.

Sizing N. Start at 10 and raise it while watching for devices that refuse or
delay logins: the limits are the switches' concurrent-session caps, the
TACACS+/RADIUS rate, and the links to remote sites  -  not the backup host.
10-25 is typical. -P applies to fetching only and cannot be combined with the
lookup/maintenance modes, which stay single-process.

On native Windows there is no fork, so -P always runs sequentially (N>1 is
reduced to -P 1); see Platform support.

Web UI backups (TP-Link Easy Smart, ProCurve 1700)
==================================================

Some switches have no CLI at all  -  TP-Link Easy Smart switches (TL-SG105E,
TL-SG105PE and siblings) and the HP ProCurve 1700 series. The tplink-web-sg105e
and procurve-web-1700 models retrieve the config the same way a browser does on
the switch's config backup page: they log in over plain HTTP (the switches offer
no HTTPS), download the config file, and log out again. The two models share
their HTTP plumbing (AbstractHTTP.pm); the login, download and logout requests
are per device, taken from packet captures of real browser sessions.

TP-Link: both session mechanisms seen on real devices are handled  -  the
TL-SG105E binds the login session to the client's IP address, the TL-SG105PE v2
uses a session cookie. Either way the switch only allows a limited number of
concurrent logins; if it reports the login table as full, the backup of that
device is reported as failed rather than forcing another user out.

ProCurve 1700: the switch has only a manager password (user is ignored) and
binds the session to the client's IP address.

    WARNING: Secrets in clear
    The 1700's switch.cfg contains the SNMP communities and, apparently, the
    manager password in clear  -  protect the repository accordingly.

The downloaded config is an opaque binary file, not text. It is stored uuencoded
(begin 644 <name>.cfg ... end) so that it fits the text-based repository, the
changes_only comparison and the -g retrieval like any other backup. To turn a
backup back into the file the switch's restore page expects:

    uudecode -o config.cfg <backup-file>
    # or straight from the repository:
    fetchconfig.pl -devices=... -g <dev_id> | uudecode -o config.cfg

    NOTE: Diffs are not meaningful here
    A line-by-line comparison between two backups (-m) works, but on uuencoded
    binary it only shows that something changed, not what. Byte-exact change
    detection (changes_only=1) is unaffected: identical binaries encode to
    identical text.

ProCurve SNMP backups (ProVision, 4000M/8000M)
==============================================

The older HP ProCurve ProVision chassis (4000M, 8000M) can only be managed
remotely through a VT100 menu, which is not usable for scripted backup. They
will, however, export a text copy of their configuration over TFTP when told to
over SNMP, and that is what procurve-snmp does. It is a transport unlike the
other models  -  no shell and no web UI, but SNMP plus TFTP  -  and so has no
user/pass: the credential is the SNMP read-write community.

An SNMP set arms the switch's TFTP export, the config is fetched from the switch
by TFTP, and a second SNMP set disables the export again. That disable always
runs, even when the transfer failed, so the switch is never left with its TFTP
server open.

    NOTE: browse vs. running-config
    The default remote_file=browse produces a human-readable report  -  fine for
    backup and change tracking, but a display export, not a file that can be
    TFTP'd back to restore the switch. Later ProVision models also accept
    remote_file=running-config, a replayable config.

    WARNING: TFTP is inbound
    The switch pushes the file to the backup host over TFTP (UDP/69 inbound,
    then a data port). A host firewall on the backup machine can block this; it
    then shows up as a TFTP get failure or timeout, not a hang.

Retrieving a previously backed-up config
========================================

In addition to fetching configuration from devices, fetchconfig.pl can retrieve
a configuration file already saved in the repository, without contacting the
device again  -  useful for scripting: dumping the latest config to stdout,
comparing today's config against yesterday's, or pulling an older backup by its
position in the history. The examples below walk through retrieval and the
related repository-maintenance modes; the full, grouped option reference follows
under Flag reference.

1. Retrieve the latest backup
-----------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net

2. Retrieve an older backup
---------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 2

3. List every backup on file
----------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -l fw01.acme.net

Sample output (tab-separated: number, size in bytes, path). The numbers in the
first column are exactly what -n/-m expect (1=latest, 2=next most recent, ...).

    1	28221	/usr/local/fetchconfig/config/202608/20260824/fw01.acme.net/fw01.acme.net.run.20260824.001105CEST
    2	28221	/usr/local/fetchconfig/config/202608/20260823/fw01.acme.net/fw01.acme.net.run.20260823.001105CEST

4. Check for 0-byte backups
---------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -z fw01.acme.net

Catches a fetch that connected but failed to save anything. If any are found,
they're listed like -l above and fetchconfig.pl exits 1; if none are found,
nothing is printed and the exit status is 0.

5. Check every device at once
-----------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -Z

Same as -z, but across every loaded device in one pass (tab-separated: dev_id,
number, size, path). A device whose repository can't be read is logged and
skipped without aborting the rest of the check.

6. Delete 0-byte backups
------------------------

Add -D to delete the empty backups -z/-Z find  -  only the empty backup files
(never directories), and only for devices still in the table, since -z/-Z look
only at loaded devices (a decommissioned device's leftovers are orphaned files,
cleaned with -o below). Preview first with -T: the deletes are logged as would
delete: unlink <path>, nothing is removed.

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -z fw01.acme.net -D -T    # preview
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -z fw01.acme.net -D       # delete

An emptied day/month directory is left in place; prune it afterwards with -e -D.
The exit status is unchanged by -D  -  still 1 if any 0-byte backup was found, 0
otherwise.

7. Find orphaned backups
------------------------

Configs on disk with no matching device in the table  -  e.g. left behind by a
decommissioned or renamed device.

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -o

-o also lists a removed device's <dev_id>.status and <dev_id>.debug files at the
repository root (not inside the dated directory); with -D they are deleted too.
This clears a decommissioned device's debug log, which can hold credentials. To
delete what -o found, add -D. Since this is destructive, preview it first with
-T  -  the delete commands are only logged, never run:

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -o -D -T    # preview
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -o -D       # delete for real

8. Find empty directories
-------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -e            # list
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -e -D -T      # preview delete
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -e -D         # delete

-e finds empty directories left behind in the repositories (e.g. after deleting
old backups by hand); with -D it removes them bottom-up. See the Flag reference
for the full semantics.

9. Check suffix consistency
---------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -s fw01.acme.net   # one device
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -S                 # every device

Checks whether a device's backups carry inconsistent filename suffixes (e.g.
after filename_append_suffix was introduced on a device that already had
backups). See the Flag reference.

10. Save to a file instead of stdout
------------------------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 2 -f config.file

11. Compare two backups
-----------------------

    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net -n 1 -m 2

Output matches the standard diff older newer convention: lines starting with <
come from -m (older), lines starting with > come from -n (newer).

Not available on native Windows: the comparison shells out to an external diff
and is disabled there  -  it exits with an error instead of a diff. See Platform
support.

    NOTE: stdout stays clean
    Log/diagnostic messages always go to stderr, so stdout only ever carries the
    retrieved config content  -  safe to pipe and redirect, e.g.
    ./fetchconfig.pl -devices=device_table -g fw01.acme.net > latest.cfg.

The device-unique-id passed to -g (or -l) is looked up in the device table(s)
given with -devices= and/or -line=, exactly as when fetching; at least one of
-devices= or -line= is therefore still required. Only the device's options
(model, repository, etc.) are resolved  -  the device itself is not contacted.

Flag reference
--------------

Retrieval options
-----------------

Retrieval mode for dev_id instead of fetching from every device in the table(s).
dev_id must match a device line's dev-unique-id. Plain retrieval works on all
platforms; the compare form (-g ... -m) is not available on native Windows (see
Platform support).

Listing mode: prints one line per backup on file, newest first  -
<number>\t<size>\t<path>.

Selects which backup to retrieve, counting from the most recent: N=1 latest
(default), N=2 the one before that, and so on. Selecting a backup works on all
platforms; only the -m comparison it feeds is unavailable on native Windows.

Compare mode: prints a diff between backup M (older) and backup N (newer)
instead of printing backup N. M must be numerically higher than N. On native
Windows this is disabled (it needs an external diff) and exits with an error.

- 0 | the two backups are identical
- 1 | the two backups differ -- OR an in-mode error prevented the compare (a
  missing backup N/M, or diff could not run); on Linux/AIX these are not
  distinguished from "differ"
- 2 | native Windows only: compare is unsupported (it needs an external diff)

Housekeeping options
--------------------

Zero-byte check for one device, same tab-separated format as -l. Nothing prints
if no backups are 0 bytes.

- 1 | at least one 0-byte backup found
- 0 | all backups non-empty

Same as -z, no dev_id  -  checks every loaded device:
<dev_id>\t<number>\t<size>\t<path>. A device that can't be scanned is skipped
and logged, and does not by itself cause a non-zero exit.

Orphaned-backup check: lists device directories on disk with no matching dev_id
in the currently loaded table(s)  -  <name>\t<file count>\t<total
bytes>\t<path>. Scans every repository= path configured by a currently loaded
device (deduplicated); a path used only by a device no longer in the table can't
be discovered this way.

- 1 | at least one orphan found (backup dir or status/debug file)
- 0 | none found

Empty-directory check: scans every configured repository= path for empty
directories in the three-level layout <repository>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<dev_id>/ and
lists them, one tab-separated row per directory: level (device, day or month)
and path. The scan is bottom-up, so a day whose only content is an empty device
directory, and a month whose only content is such a day, are listed too. Only
names of the expected shape are candidates (YYYYMM; YYYYMMDD whose first six
digits match the month; a dev_id-shaped name starting with a letter or digit)  -
anything else, and the repository root, is never touched. Such leftovers
typically come from deleting old backups by hand. Honours -f.

With -D the directories are deleted, bottom-up: device dirs, then the day dirs
empty by then, then the month dirs empty by then. Each removal is one plain
rmdir on the exact path  -  no wildcards, no recursion; rmdir refuses a
non-empty directory, so files can never be taken along. Every command is
reported as deleting: rmdir <path>; rows gain a deleted/failed column. -e -D -T
reports would delete: rmdir <path> and removes nothing.

- 0 | -e: no empty directories * -e -D: every rmdir succeeded
- 1 | -e: empty directories found * -e -D: at least one rmdir failed

With -e: deletes the empty directories found (see -e). With -o: also deletes
every orphaned device directory -o finds (and the orphaned <dev_id>.status /
<dev_id>.debug files at the repository root), and since v9.56 then prunes the
day (YYYYMMDD) and month (YYYYMM) directories this leaves empty  -  one plain
rmdir each, only if empty, each reported. Each backup file is deleted
individually by its exact name  -  never a wildcard, never rm -rf  -  logged as
rm <file>, followed by rmdir <path> for the now-empty directory. Deletion uses
Perl's own unlink()/rmdir(); no shell command is actually spawned. rmdir() only
succeeds on a genuinely empty directory, so anything left behind unexpectedly
makes that removal fail loudly rather than being swept away. A repository root
itself is never deleted. With -z or -Z: deletes the 0-byte backup files found  -
one plain unlink on each exact path (never a directory), logged as deleting:
unlink <path>; an emptied directory is left for a later -e -D to prune. -D
requires one of -o, -e, -z or -Z.

- 0 | -o/-e: nothing needed deleting, or every removal succeeded
- 1 | -o/-e: a removal failed * -z/-Z: a 0-byte backup was found (the check's
  own result; deleting it does not change the exit)

Preview mode for -o -D, -e -D, -z -D and -Z -D: logs the delete commands -D
would run (would delete: rmdir/unlink <path>), without deleting anything. Also
valid with --clean-lock-dir (shows which lock files would be removed) and with
--db-backfill (previews the counts without hashing or writing). Exit status is
that of the plain check  -  -o: 1 if anything orphaned was found; -e: 1 if any
empty directory was found; -z/-Z: 1 if any 0-byte backup was found; 0 otherwise.
Use it before a bare -D run.

Suffix-consistency check for one device: verifies that all of its backup files
carry the same filename suffix. Reports the device if its backups mix suffixes
-  including "some with a suffix and some without", which is what happens when
filename_append_suffix was added or changed after backups already existed.
Prints one tab-separated line per distinct suffix: dev_id, suffix (or (none)),
count, example file. Prints nothing if consistent. An unknown dev_id exits 1.
Honours -f.

A second step then compares the (single) suffix the backups use against the
filename_append_suffix configured for the device and logs a warning if they
differ  -  e.g. backups written before a suffix was configured. This step is
skipped for a device whose backups are already inconsistent.

- 0 | backup suffixes are consistent, configured suffix matches backup file
  suffixes
- 1 | inconsistent backup suffixes (not checked against the configured suffix),
  or unknown device
- 2 | backup suffixes are consistent, but the configured suffix does not match
  them

Like -s, but takes no dev_id and checks every device loaded via
-devices=/-line=. -S is a pure consistency check  -  it does not compare against
the configured filename_append_suffix (that second step is -s dev_id only). Exit
1 if any device's backups mix suffixes, 0 otherwise. Note -s takes a dev_id:
write -s dev_id -f file or -S -f file; -s -f file is rejected.

    NOTE: How the suffix check separates the timezone from the suffix
    A backup is named <dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS<tz><suffix>, and <tz> is
    whatever strftime produced on the backup host  -  it differs by platform and
    changes with daylight saving time (AIX: CET in winter, CEST in summer; older
    AIX NFT/DFT). The check parses the tz token first  -  numeric +0200/-0500
    (Linux, modern Windows), a bare name CEST (AIX), -CEST (Solaris), or a
    multi-word name such as W. Europe Standard Time (older Windows)  -  and only
    then looks at what follows, so a DST change is not reported as a suffix
    change. It only works if the suffix is separated from the tz by ., - or _: .
    is the rule since v9.55; - and _ are accepted so backups written before that
    rule are still recognized. One inherent ambiguity: - followed by uppercase
    letters is the Solaris tz form, so an old suffix like -BAK on a
    timezone=hide backup (no tz token) is read as a tz; _BAK, -bak, or any
    suffix after a real tz token are unambiguous.

Template options (generic model)
--------------------------------

Lists the generic-model templates available to the loaded devices, one
TAB-separated row per template: <id> <section> <full_path> <model>
<template_dir>. section is default (a template in the directory the default:
scope uses  -  the default: generic template_dir= if set, else the program
default templates/ beside fetchconfig.pl; feeds the defaults editor) or device
(a template in a device-specific template_dir=; feeds the device editor).
template_dir is the configured value as written (a directory: $TAG or a literal
path), so a consumer can filter by exactly what the table holds; full_path is
always the real resolved path (a $TAG is expanded). A device configuring the
same path as the default still yields device rows, so a model may appear under
both sections; device directories are deduplicated by configured path (no device
id in the output). Rows are sorted default first, then device; within a section
by directory, then model. Every directory to be scanned  -  the default included
-  must exist; a missing configured path is reported and the exit status is 2
regardless of what else was found, otherwise 0 if any template was found, 1 if
none. Honours -f. See Template-driven model.

Lists the directory: allowlist configured in the device table (see Directory
allowlist), one TAB-separated row per entry: <id> <type> <tag> <path>. It also
lints: exit 0 if a directory: section exists and every entry is valid
(well-formed and its path exists on disk), 1 if no section, 2 if any entry is
invalid.

Structurally checks the generic-model template at PATH (grammar + the load-time
validator) and exits. PATH must be the full path to the .tmpl file  -  a bare
name is not resolved, as the same name can exist in different template_dir
directories. Prints ok or the errors; exits 0 ok, 1 errors, 2 file problem.
Structural check only. Does not honour -f. It needs no device table and does not
apply the directory: allowlist  -  any readable .tmpl path is checked.

Prints the syntax style a generic-model template declares  -  the value of a #
syntax_style: <token> header comment  -  to stdout, and exits. This is metadata
for a config viewer (e.g. fetchconfig-web) to pick a highlighter for a
generic-model backup, whose generic model name says nothing about the output
syntax; it has no effect on a backup. PATH is the full path to the .tmpl file,
as for --check-template. Exits 0 and prints the token if declared, 1 and prints
nothing if not, 2 on a file problem (error to stderr). Log output stays on
stderr, so stdout carries only the value. It needs no device table and does not
apply the directory: allowlist  -  any readable .tmpl path is read. The value is
a free-text token [A-Za-z0-9_-]+. fetchconfig-web currently recognises
cisco-ios, procurve, comware, zyxel, aruba-cx, nexus, mediant, template, json,
xml and generic; the set is open, and any other or absent value lets the viewer
fall back to plain text.

Fetching options
----------------

Fetch with N concurrent worker processes (default 1 = sequential, device-table
order, live output). With N>1 each device's output is buffered while it runs and
shown as one uninterrupted block when it finishes, in completion order  -  never
interleaved with another device. Not combinable with
-g/-l/-z/-Z/-o/-e/-s/-S/--db-backfill. See Parallel fetching. On Windows there
is no fork, so -P always runs sequentially (N>1 is reduced to 1).

Output option
-------------

Writes the result data to file instead of stdout: the retrieved config (-g), the
diff (-m), or the listing rows (-l, -z, -Z, -o, -e, -s, -S, -t,
--list-allowed-dirs). The file is created/overwritten as needed. It does not
apply to --check-template, whose ok/error result is written to the log only.

The file receives only that data  -  never the screen output. All
info:/debug:/error: lines, including the "checked N backup(s), ..." summary and
the "wrote ... to file" confirmation, are log messages and always go to stderr.

For the listing/check modes (-l, -z, -Z, -o, -e, -s, -S, -t,
--list-allowed-dirs) the result data ends with one summary line prefixed by #,
carrying the same text the run logs  -  e.g. # dev=sw01: checked 2 backup(s), no
zero-byte backups found  -  so the output is never empty and is self-describing:
a reader can tell "checked, nothing found" from "nothing was written" without
the exit status or stderr. The rows before it contain only the items found. The
trailer is part of the result stream, so stdout and -f carry identical content.
It is not added to -g or -m, which stay byte-exact.

    WARNING: Format change for parsers (v9.55)
    Anything that parses these listings  -  fetchconfig-web in particular  -
    must skip lines starting with #. Before v9.55 a check that found nothing
    produced a 0-byte file.

Maintenance options
-------------------

Removes device lock files older than N days (by mtime) from the lock directory,
then exits. With -T, only shows what would be removed (dry run). N defaults to
30, minimum 1. A lock file is removed only if it is not currently held  -  the
cleaner flock()s each candidate first and keeps any live lock  -  so an in-use
lock is never deleted. At least one -devices=/-line= is required (the
devices-required check runs before this mode); the lock directory is <dir of
fetchconfig.pl>/.lock by default, so pass the device table that configures a
custom lock_dir=/directory: lock to clean that directory instead. Exits 0 on
success, 2 on a bad N or an unusable lock directory.

Reconstructs the history database from the backups already on disk (requires a
db: line; see Backup-history database), then exits  -  use it once after first
enabling db:, since otherwise only new runs populate the DB. For every loaded
device it scans its repository= and, for each stored backup not yet in the DB,
inserts a backup_versions row (its sha1 recomputed from the file  -  identical
to what a live save stores) and a backup_runs row (state success, changed,
run_end = the backup's timestamp, no duration); it also inserts a backup_current
row for each device that has none yet, from <dev_id>.status plus the newest
backup. When the fetchconfig-web 2.12 line-delta columns exist it records
lines_added/lines_removed on each version and UPDATEs existing version rows
whose deltas are still empty. Idempotent and repeatable: an existing row is left
alone, so a re-run only fills what is still missing (and does not re-hash a
backup already recorded), and a backup_current row a live run wrote is never
overwritten. Connects up front and aborts if the DB is unreachable. With -T it
previews the counts (what would be inserted) without hashing or writing. Only
saved backups can be reconstructed: the dense run history (unchanged or failed
runs leave no file) is not on disk, so backfilled backup_runs rows are the saved
change-events only (state success, no durations)  -  the full run history
accrues from live runs afterwards. Mutually exclusive with the
lookup/maintenance modes (-g/-l/-z/-Z/-o/-e/-s/-S); -T previews it. Exits 0 on
success, 1 if the DB is unreachable or a write failed.

Renames a device id everywhere it is used, then exits: the device-table line,
the on-disk backup tree, and  -  if a db: line is set  -  the backup-history
database. Requires --old <current-id> and --new <new-id>. The new id must
satisfy the device-id rules (letters, digits, _, ., -; not all dots) and must
not already be in use  -  the mode refuses if --new is in the table, has any
on-disk backups/.status/.debug, or (when the DB is reachable) already has
history rows. The device-table file is locked for the operation (an exclusive
lock on a <devices>.lock sidecar; a concurrent run waits up to 2 s for it, then
aborts). It then: renames every dated leaf directory
<repository>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<old>/ and the <old>.run.* files inside it to the
new id, renames <repository>/<old>.status and <old>.debug, rewrites only field 2
of the device-table line (model, host, options, comment and spacing preserved)
atomically, and queues the DB change (UPDATE dev_id across
backup_current/backup_runs/backup_versions) onto the repository outbox, draining
it immediately. An interrupted rename leaves a .rename-<old>-to-<new>.inprogress
journal in the repository so re-running the same command resumes it. With -T it
previews every rename and the SQL without writing anything. The device must come
from a -devices= file (not -line=). Mutually exclusive with the other modes and
with -P. Exits 0 when fully done, 1 if the file/table rename succeeded but the
DB change was left queued because the DB was unreachable (apply it later with
--replay-db-change), 2 on a validation or name-collision refusal (nothing
changed).

Drains any pending history-DB operations from the per-repository outbox  -
device-id renames or statistics left queued while the DB was down  -  for every
loaded device's repository, then exits. No fetching. This is the lightweight
catch-up after a DB outage (a full run also drains the outbox at its end, but
this applies it without touching any device). Requires a db: line. With -T it
reports what is pending without applying it. Exits 0 if nothing remains pending
after the drain, 1 if operations are still pending (the DB is still
unreachable). Mutually exclusive with the other modes.

Generic options
---------------

Prints just the program version to stdout (one line, e.g. 9.78) and exits with
status 0. Nothing else is written  -  no banner, nothing on stderr  -  so a
script can parse it safely.

Prints the usage/help text to stdout and exits with status 0. (Before v9.55, -?
was treated as an unknown argument: it printed an error, the usage to stderr,
and ended with "Died at ...".)

    WARNING: Unknown device or missing backup
    If dev_id is unknown, or the device has no repository configured,
    fetchconfig.pl reports an error and exits non-zero; nothing is written to
    stdout or -f. Same if -n (or -m) refers to a backup that doesn't exist  -
    use -l first if unsure how many backups exist. -m shells out to the standard
    diff utility, which must be available in PATH.

Exit codes
==========

Every invocation returns a process exit status a script can test. The per-flag
cards above give each mode's codes; this is the consolidated summary.

Two codes are cross-cutting  -  they can come from any mode:

Code  Meaning
----  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
255   Usage or argument error - an unknown or invalid option, a missing value,
      an illegal mode combination, or --db-backfill with no db: line. The usage
      text is printed to stderr and the program exits.
2     The device table's directory: allowlist has an error, so the run is
      refused before any fetch or lookup. Every mode except --list-allowed-dirs,
      which reports allowlist errors itself.

Per mode (a mode not listing a code never returns it):

Mode                                 Exit status
-----------------------------------  -------------------------------------------
(no mode flag)  -  normal fetch run  0 the run completed. 10 a device's lock
                                     directory could not be created or used
                                     (misconfiguration). A per-device fetch
                                     failure is logged and does not change the
                                     exit.
-g  -  retrieve a backup             0 backup emitted. 1 unknown device, no
                                     repository, or the selected backup does not
                                     exist.
-g ... -m  -  compare two backups    0 the two backups are identical. 1 they
                                     differ, or an in-mode error prevented the
                                     compare (a missing backup, or diff could
                                     not run) - on Linux/AIX these are not
                                     distinguished from "differ". 2 native
                                     Windows only: compare is unsupported (it
                                     needs an external diff).
-l  -  list backups                  0 listed (zero or more rows). 1 unknown
                                     device or no repository.
-z / -Z  -  zero-byte check          0 all backups non-empty. 1 at least one
                                     0-byte backup found. Adding -D does not
                                     change the status.
-o  -  orphan check                  0 none found. 1 at least one orphan found.
-e  -  empty-directory check         0 none found (with -e -D, every rmdir
                                     succeeded). 1 empty directories found (with
                                     -e -D, at least one rmdir failed).
-s / -S  -  suffix check             0 suffixes are consistent and the
                                     configured suffix matches. 1 inconsistent
                                     suffixes, or an unknown device. 2
                                     consistent, but the configured suffix does
                                     not match.
-t / --list-template                 0 at least one template found. 1 none
                                     found. 2 a configured template directory is
                                     missing (regardless of what else was
                                     found).
--list-allowed-dirs                  0 a directory: section exists and every
                                     entry is valid. 1 no section. 2 an entry is
                                     invalid.
--check-template                     0 ok. 1 structural errors. 2 file problem
                                     (PATH unreadable).
--template-syntax                    0 a syntax token is declared (printed to
                                     stdout). 1 none declared (nothing printed).
                                     2 file problem (error to stderr).
--clean-lock-dir                     0 success. 2 a bad N, or an unusable lock
                                     directory. With -T it previews and writes
                                     nothing.
--db-backfill                        0 success. 1 the DB is unreachable or a
                                     write failed. With -T it previews and
                                     writes nothing.
--rename-device-id                   0 fully done. 1 the file/table rename
                                     succeeded but the DB change was left queued
                                     (DB unreachable) - apply it later with
                                     --replay-db-change. 2 a validation or
                                     name-collision refusal (nothing changed).
                                     With -T it previews and writes nothing.
--replay-db-change                   0 nothing left pending after the drain. 1
                                     operations are still pending (DB
                                     unreachable). With -T it reports what is
                                     pending and applies nothing.
-v / --version, -h / -? / --help     0 printed, then exit.

-n, -f, -P, -D and -T are modifiers, not modes: the exit status is that of the
mode they run under. With -D the status still reflects the check's own finding
(-z -D stays 1 if a 0-byte backup was found); -T only previews and writes
nothing.

Options reference
=================

The modules CiscoIOS, CiscoCAT, CiscoASA, and CiscoPIX recognize the following
options; nearly every other model reuses the same set (see the per-model matrix
below for exactly which apply). The template-driven generic model has its own
additional options  -  see Per-model option tables.

Option                  Requirement  Description
----------------------  -----------  -------------------------------------------
pass                    Mandatory    The login password.
user                    Optional     Login username. Can be omitted for devices
                                     not requiring one.
enable                  Mandatory    The enable password.
timeout                 Mandatory    How long to wait for TELNET responses from
                                     devices.
fetch_timeout           Optional     Overrides timeout specifically while
                                     fetching configuration lines - useful for
                                     devices that take several seconds to build
                                     the configuration.
repository              Mandatory    Base directory for saving configuration
                                     files.
keep                    Mandatory    Maximum number of config files to retain
                                     per device; older files are discarded once
                                     the limit is reached.
changes_only            Optional     Boolean. If changes_only=1, only new
                                     configurations are saved. Otherwise the
                                     script may retain multiple identical files
                                     across runs.
on_fetch_run            Optional     Path to an external program called whenever
                                     a config is fetched. Passed
                                     FETCHCONFIG_DEV_ID, FETCHCONFIG_DEV_HOST,
                                     FETCHCONFIG_PREV (undefined if none), and
                                     FETCHCONFIG_CURR. Available for all
                                     modules. If changes_only=1, the file at
                                     FETCHCONFIG_CURR is erased right after the
                                     program finishes. The value is a program
                                     plus optional literal arguments, not a
                                     shell command line: since v9.68 it is split
                                     into words (quoting is honoured, e.g. prog
                                     "two words") and executed directly, never
                                     through /bin/sh, so shell syntax such as ;,
                                     |, $(...) or > is passed to the program as
                                     plain text. To run a pipeline, put it in a
                                     script and name the script. The program may
                                     not live in a system directory (/bin,
                                     /usr/lib, /etc, ... - see Directory
                                     allowlist).
on_fetch_cat            Optional     Boolean. If on_fetch_cat=1, the fetched
                                     configuration is also written to stdout.
                                     Available for all modules.
timezone                Optional     If timezone=hide, suppresses timezone
                                     information from the config filename.
filename_append_suffix  Optional     Appended to the generated backup filename,
                                     which then reads
                                     <dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS<tz><suffix>,
                                     e.g. filename_append_suffix=.txt. Available
                                     for all modules. fetchconfig 9.x and later
                                     only accept a suffix that starts with a "."
                                     (a file extension); a dotless suffix, or
                                     one containing / or whitespace, fails that
                                     device's backup with an error and writes
                                     nothing. Do not add, remove or change the
                                     suffix once a device has backups - files
                                     are found by their <dev_id>.run. prefix, so
                                     old and new names would coexist (keep=N
                                     expires them together, "latest" ordering
                                     can be affected). Decide before the first
                                     backup; use -s dev_id or -S to find devices
                                     whose existing backups already mix
                                     suffixes.
to                      Optional     Overrides, for this device only, who
                                     receives the backup summary e-mail. See
                                     E-mail notifications and Change reports.
                                     Available for all modules.
comment                 Optional     Free-text note about the device, for
                                     whoever reads the device table - not used
                                     anywhere else (not logged, not e-mailed,
                                     not saved into the backup file). Available
                                     for all modules. Example: comment=lab
                                     switch.
site                    Optional     Free-text site/location tag, like comment -
                                     fetchconfig does not use it. Used by
                                     fetchconfig-web for per-site access control
                                     (users see only devices whose site matches
                                     their user record; a device with no site is
                                     visible only to "all sites" / site 0
                                     users). Single-byte characters. Example:
                                     site="Plant 3, Hall A".
lock                    Optional     lock=on takes an exclusive flock on the
                                     device before backing it up, so two runs
                                     cannot back up the same device at once
                                     (overlapping cron, or a web "Backup Now"
                                     over a cron run). Values are on or off;
                                     default off. The lock releases when the run
                                     ends (even on crash) and the lock file is
                                     never deleted. On contention the device is
                                     retried (see lock_retry/lock_retry_time)
                                     and then recorded as skipped; the process
                                     exit code is not changed.
lock_dir                Optional     Directory for the lock files - a directory:
                                     lock $TAG or an absolute path. Default <dir
                                     of fetchconfig.pl>/.lock, created 0700 if
                                     absent; if it cannot be created or used,
                                     fetchconfig exits 10 after the run. Must be
                                     a local filesystem (flock over NFS on AIX
                                     is unreliable); a system directory is
                                     refused.
lock_retry              Optional     How many times to retry a locked device
                                     before skipping. Integer >= 0; default 5. 0
                                     = skip on first contention.
lock_retry_time         Optional     Seconds between lock retries. Integer >= 1;
                                     default 10. Maximum wait before skipping is
                                     lock_retry x lock_retry_time seconds.

    NOTE: Quoting option values
    Any option value may be wrapped in double quotes, e.g. comment="Building 3,
    Floor 2". This is only needed if the value contains a literal comma, since a
    comma otherwise separates one option from the next  -  quoting is never
    required just because a value contains spaces. Inside a quoted value, \" and
    \\ represent a literal quote and backslash. Quoting is optional, not
    enforced: an unquoted value with a comma is still just split at that comma,
    exactly as before.

Per-model option matrix
-----------------------

Beyond the options above, each model recognizes its own subset of user / pass /
enable / timeout / repository / keep / fetch_timeout / changes_only.
on_fetch_run, on_fetch_cat, timezone, filename_append_suffix, to, comment, site,
the lock... options and debug are available for every model and are omitted here
for space.

Model                   user  pass  enable  timeout  repository  keep  fetch_timeout  changes_only
----------------------  ----  ----  ------  -------  ----------  ----  -------------  ------------
3com-msr                yes   yes   yes     opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
acme                    opt.  yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
aruba-cx-ssh[^1]        yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-asa               yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-asa-ssh           yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-cat               yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-ios               yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-ios-ssh           yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-iosxr             yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-pix               yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
cisco-sg300             yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
comware-ssh             yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
coriant-8600            yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
dell                    yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
dmswitch                yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
fortigate               yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
generic[^2]             opt.  yes   opt.    opt.     yes         opt.  opt.           opt.
hirschmann[^3]          yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
junos                   yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
mediant                 yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
mediant-sbc[^4]         yes   yes   yes     opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
mikrotik                yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
nec-univerge-ix         yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
nexus-ssh[^5]           yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
parks[^6]               yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
planet-ssh[^7]          yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
procurve                yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
procurve-snmp[^8]        -     -     -      opt.     yes         yes    -             opt.
procurve-ssh            yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
procurve-web-1700[^9]    -    yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
riverstone              yes   yes   yes     opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
tellabs-msr             yes   yes   yes     opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
terayon-os              yes   yes   yes     opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
tplink-web-sg105e[^10]  yes   yes    -      opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.
zyxel[^11]              yes   yes   opt.    opt.     yes         yes   opt.           opt.

timeout is shown as optional for every model because it has a built-in default:
if it is set neither on the device line nor on the model's default: line,
fetchconfig uses 30 seconds and logs Using 30s fetch timeout because the timeout
was not set for this model at debug level. Before v9.55 an unset timeout fell
through to the underlying library's own default (10 s for telnet/SSH, 180 s for
the web-UI models), so set it explicitly for anything but a lab test.

[^1]: aruba-cx-ssh: user must be an administrator (AOS-CX has no enable; an
operator's > prompt is an error). Also works for HPE Aruba Networking AOS-8
wireless access points (confirmed on an AP model 515, AOS-8 8.13.2.2). Pager
disabled with no page; the Current configuration: header is dropped. Also
recognizes banner_timeout, show_cmd, prompt_settle_ms (idle-wait before matching
the prompt, default 150 ms  -  the prompt token allows spaces and non-ASCII, and
the wait keeps a banner line ending in "#" from being taken for the prompt) and
debug (same debug file layout as planet-ssh).
[^2]: generic is the template-driven model: model= names the template and is
mandatory (it may be set on a default: generic line so devices sharing one
template need not repeat it, and a device line can still override it), transport
is ssh/telnet, and user/pass are mandatory for SSH. It recognizes many more
timing and prompt options (most also settable in the template)  -  see Per-model
option tables for the full list and defaults.
[^3]: hirschmann's enable is optional because that firmware's enable command
doesn't ask for a password; it's read defensively in case another firmware
version does. hirschmann also recognizes show_cmd (optional; replaces show
running-config all). The re-salted users passwd <user> :v1:<hash>: field  -
leading 18 hex (9 bytes) password-derived and stable, trailing hex a random salt
regenerated on every read  -  is canonicalised before the changes_only
comparison: the salt is collapsed and the prefix kept, so salt churn alone no
longer forces a save while a real password change (which alters the prefix)
still does. The saved backup keeps the exact hash; the report/e-mail masking is
unchanged. The generic model offers the same via the template directive canon
/PATTERN/REPLACEMENT/.
[^4]: mediant-sbc also recognizes transport (optional; ssh default, telnet, or
auto = SSH with telnet fallback only when no SSH service answers),
prompt_settle_ms (optional, SSH; idle-wait before matching the prompt, default
150 ms), banner_timeout, pager_cmd (optional; a command sent before the show
command to disable paging, for firmware that offers one  -  its rejection, e.g.
"Invalid command" or "%", aborts the fetch; not needed on the observed Mediant
500Li) and debug (optional). Its show_cmd (optional) defaults to show
running-config  -  the settings that differ from firmware defaults, the form the
device's own CLI-script export produces and that a restore via copy cli-script
from ... expects; show running-config full lists every parameter including
defaults (better for auditing default changes across firmware upgrades, riskier
to replay on newer firmware). To keep both, add a second device entry with its
own dev_id and show_cmd="show running-config full". The CLI pauses with
--MORE-- after each screen; the pager is driven (a space is sent at each marker
and the marker and its erase sequence are removed from the saved configuration)
without entering configuration mode.
[^5]: nexus-ssh: user must land on the # prompt (NX-OS has no enable; a > prompt
is an error). Pager disabled with terminal length 0. The !Time: header line of
the running-config changes on every run and is ignored by changes_only; !Running
configuration last done at: counts. Also recognizes banner_timeout, show_cmd,
prompt_settle_ms (idle-wait before matching the prompt, default 150 ms) and
debug (on/off, default off; records to <repository>/<dev_id>.debug, mode 0600,
the ssh authentication record followed by the raw switch session).
[^6]: parks also recognizes banner_timeout (optional)  -  overrides the timeout
used to wait for the initial login: prompt, since the Parks 209R can take many
seconds to issue it.
[^7]: planet-ssh: user/pass serve the SSH authentication and, on firmware that
shows one, the switch's own Username:/Password: dialog that follows it. enable
is optional and only used if the account lands on the > prompt; it defaults to
the login password. The ! System Up Time: header line of the running-config is
kept in the backup but ignored by changes_only. Also recognizes banner_timeout,
show_cmd, debug (to <repository>/<dev_id>.debug: first the ssh client's verbose
account of the SSH authentication, then the raw switch session  -  the SSH login
itself is not switch output, so it can only appear as those ssh log lines) and
type_delay (milliseconds between typed characters, default 100  -  the switch
does not reliably accept a burst of input right after a prompt, so every line is
typed character by character; 0 disables the pauses).
[^8]: procurve-snmp has no user/pass: the credential is the SNMP read-write
community (masked in logs). timeout defaults to 30 s and covers both the SNMP
session and the TFTP transfer; also recognizes oid (the copy/TFTP-mode trigger
OID; default the one used by the observed chassis), remote_file (the TFTP
filename to fetch, default browse), snmp_version (default snmpv2c) and debug
(records the SNMP sets and the TFTP transfer).
[^9]: procurve-web-1700 likewise talks HTTP; it has only a manager password, so
user is ignored. Same timeout/fetch_timeout/debug handling as tplink-web-sg105e.
[^10]: tplink-web-sg105e talks HTTP, not telnet: user/pass are the web UI login,
timeout applies to each HTTP request, and fetch_timeout to the config download
request only. Also recognizes debug (optional, boolean  -  records every HTTP
request and response of the run, password masked and binary bodies summarized by
size, to <repository>/<dev_id>.debug).
[^11]: zyxel's enable is optional: one real login sample went straight to the
privileged prompt with no separate "enable" step (account already had full
privilege), while another needed "enable" plus its own password  -  both are
handled, so this is read defensively rather than assumed either way. zyxel also
recognizes show_cmd (optional, replaces show running-config) and debug
(optional, boolean  -  records the raw telnet session to
<repository>/<dev_id>.debug when set to on, same convention as
procurve/procurve-ssh).

Per-model option tables (detailed)
----------------------------------

The matrix above is the overview; the tables below are the full per-model
reference  -  the exact options each model accepts and whether each is mandatory
or optional. (fetchconfig-web parses these tables to build its device-table
editor.) Option meanings are described once in the common options table above;
model-specific notes follow each table.

generic (template-driven)
-------------------------

The generic module recognizes the options below. Most of the timing and prompt
options may also be set in the template file, and a device-table option
overrides the template value of the same name. The options shared with all
models are described in the common options table above.

Option            Description
----------------  --------------------------------------------------------------
model             Mandatory. The template to load: templates/<model>.tmpl (e.g.
                  model=cisco-ios). The only option with no template-file
                  equivalent. May be set on a default: generic line so all
                  generic devices sharing one template need not repeat it; a
                  device line can still override it.
transport         ssh or telnet. If omitted, the template's first-listed
                  transport is used. On native Windows only telnet works (see
                  Platform support).
user              Login username. Mandatory for SSH; for telnet it is sent at
                  the device's Username: prompt if the template's login sequence
                  uses it.
pass              Login password. Mandatory for SSH; for telnet as above.
enable            Enable password, if the template enters enable mode.
timeout           Optional, default 30 seconds. May be set in the template.
fetch_timeout     Optional. Timeout for reading the show output. May be set in
                  the template.
banner_timeout    Optional, default 30. Seconds to wait for the first prompt
                  after connecting. May be set in the template.
fetch_delay       Optional, default 0. Seconds to pause after connecting before
                  interacting. May be set in the template.
max_visits        Optional, default 8. Global cap on how many times a template
                  state may be re-entered (bounds self-loops). May be set in the
                  template.
strip_ansi        Optional, yes/no (default no). Strip VT100/ANSI escape
                  sequences from device output. May be set in the template.
prompt_head       Optional. Regex matched before the learned hostname when
                  building the prompt pattern. May be set in the template.
prompt_tail       Optional, default #. Regex matched after the learned hostname.
                  May be set in the template.
ssh_extra_opts    Optional. Extra options passed to the ssh client (e.g. legacy
                  key-exchange/cipher settings for old switches). May be set in
                  the template.
prompt_settle_ms  Optional, default 0 (off). Milliseconds to wait for the
                  channel to go idle before matching the prompt - for SSH CLIs
                  whose login banner races the prompt. May be set in the
                  template. Added to the generic engine in v9.74; it is the real
                  settle control and is distinct from the no-op prompt_settle
                  directive removed in v9.72.
type_delay_ms     Optional, default 0 (off). Milliseconds between typed
                  characters; each send (and secret) is typed char-by-char - for
                  devices that drop fast/pasted input. May be set in the
                  template.
show_cmd          Optional. The config command sent by a template's send
                  show_cmd step (e.g. show_cmd=wrterm -> write term). May be set
                  in the template.
template_dir      Optional. Directory to load the template from. An absolute
                  path, or relative to the current working directory. Defaults
                  to the templates/ directory beside fetchconfig.pl (located
                  regardless of the working directory), so this is only needed
                  to load templates from elsewhere.

    NOTE: Template-only directives
    The following are set only in the template file, not on a device line:
    capture_from (regex marking where the saved config starts), skip_first (drop
    the first N captured lines) and canon (rewrite part of each line before the
    changes_only comparison, e.g. to collapse a re-salted hash so only a real
    change counts). See README.generic-model for the full template grammar.

cisco-ios, cisco-cat, cisco-asa, cisco-pix
------------------------------------------

The cisco-ios, cisco-cat, cisco-asa and cisco-pix modules (CiscoIOS, CiscoCAT,
CiscoASA and CiscoPIX) recognize the following options:

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
pass                    A mandatory option, specifies the login password.
user                    Specifies the login username. It can be omitted for
                        devices not requiring a login username.
enable                  Mandatory, specifies the enable password.
timeout                 How long, in seconds, to wait for responses from the
                        device. Defaults to 30 seconds if not set on the device
                        line or the model's "default:" line; the default is
                        applied by fetchconfig itself and logged at debug level
                        ("Using 30s fetch timeout because the timeout was not
                        set for this model"). Before 9.55 an unset timeout fell
                        through to the underlying library's own default (10 s
                        for telnet/ SSH via Net::Telnet, 180 s for the web-UI
                        models), so set it explicitly for anything but a lab
                        test.
fetch_timeout           Optional. If given, will override the timeout value used
                        to fetch all the configuration lines. It is useful for
                        devices which spend several seconds building the
                        configuration.
repository              Mandatory, specifies the base directory for saving the
                        configuration files.
keep                    Mandatory, the maximum number of config files to retain
                        for the device. When this limit is reached, the older
                        files are discarded.
changes_only            Optional. Boolean. If specified as changes_only=1, only
                        new configurations are saved. Otherwise, configurations
                        are saved whenever the script runs; if not defined as
                        changes_only=1, the script might possibly retain
                        multiple identical configuration files.
on_fetch_run            Optional. Path to an external program to be called
                        whenever the configuration is fetched. The following
                        environmental variables are passed to the program:
                        FETCHCONFIG_DEV_ID = device unique id
                        FETCHCONFIG_DEV_HOST = device hostname (or IP addr)
                        FETCHCONFIG_PREV = pathname to previous config,
                        undefined if none exists FETCHCONFIG_CURR = pathname to
                        current config Example:
                        on_fetch_run=/usr/local/bin/mail_diff_to_admin.sh Please
                        notice that, if changes_only=1, the backup file
                        referenced to FETCHCONFIG_CURR will be erased right
                        after the external program is finished. This option is
                        available for all modules. The value is a PROGRAM plus
                        optional literal arguments, not a shell command line:
                        since 9.68 it is split into words (quoting is honoured,
                        e.g. prog "two words") and the program is executed
                        directly, never through /bin/sh. Shell syntax such as
                        ";", "/", "$(...)" or ">" is passed to the program as
                        ordinary text and has no effect. To run a shell
                        pipeline, put it in a script and name the script here.
                        The program must not live in a system directory (/bin,
                        /sbin, /usr/lib, /etc, ... - see Directory allowlist);
                        such a value is rejected.
on_fetch_cat            Optional. Boolean. If specified as on_fetch_cat=1, the
                        currently fetched configuration is issued to standard
                        output. This option is available for all modules.
timezone                Optional. If defined as timezone=hide, it will supress
                        timezone information from config filename.
filename_append_suffix  Optional. If defined, it is appended to the generated
                        backup filename, which then reads
                        <dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS<tz><suffix>. This option is
                        available for all modules. Example:
                        filename_append_suffix=.txt

Options specific to CiscoIOS:

Option    Description
--------  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
show_cmd  Optional. If specified, its value will replace the usual "show run"
          command. This is necessary for some older devices. It may be useful to
          send alternative commands to capture the configuration. To keep
          compatibility with previous versions of fetchconfig, if specified as
          show_cmd=wrterm, "write term" will be used in place of the usual "show
          run" command.

