hirschmann
----------

The hirschmann module recognizes the following options: (See above
cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions.)

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
user                    Mandatory.
pass                    Mandatory.
enable                  Optional. The Hirschmann "enable" command does not ask
                        for a password on the firmware this module was written
                        against (confirmed from a real login session) - "enable"
                        goes straight from the ">" prompt to the "#" prompt.
                        This option only matters if some other Hirschmann
                        firmware version does prompt for one; it's read
                        defensively, the same way CiscoIOS.pm handles Cisco's
                        own enable password.
repository              Mandatory.
keep                    Mandatory.
timeout                 Optional, default 30 seconds (see the cisco-ios/
                        cisco-cat section).
fetch_timeout           Optional.
changes_only            Optional.
on_fetch_run            Optional.
on_fetch_cat            Optional.
timezone                Optional.
filename_append_suffix  Optional.
show_cmd                Optional. If specified, its value will replace the usual
                        "show running-config all" command.

Note: "show running-config all" renders the "users passwd <user> :v1:<hash>:"
field differently on every read. The leading 18 hex characters (9 bytes) are
derived from the password and are stable; the trailing hex is a random salt
regenerated on every read. fetchconfig canonicalises that salt before the
changes_only comparison (keeping the password-derived prefix), so salt churn
alone no longer forces a save, while a real password change - which alters the
prefix - is still detected and saved. The SAVED backup is byte-exact (the real
hash is kept); only the comparison uses the canonical form, and the
change-report/e-mail masking is unchanged (the whole :v1: hash is shown as
:v1:****:). The 18-hex boundary is empirical (firmware L2E-06.0.03) and scoped
to ":v1:".

