----------------------

The procurve and procurve-ssh modules recognize the following options: (See
above cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions. procurve is telnet,
procurve-ssh is the SSH counterpart; the session handling is otherwise the same.
Both accept a hostname containing spaces in the prompt.)

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
user                    Mandatory.
pass                    Mandatory.
enable                  Optional. The manager/enable password, sent if the
                        account lands on the operator (">") prompt.
repository              Mandatory.
keep                    Mandatory.
timeout                 Optional, default 30 seconds (see the cisco-ios/
                        cisco-cat section).
fetch_timeout           Optional.
changes_only            Optional.
on_fetch_run            Optional.
on_fetch_cat            Optional.
timezone                Optional.
filename_append_suffix  Optional.
debug                   Optional. Boolean. If specified as debug=on (default
                        off), records the raw session to
                        <repository>/<dev_id>.debug for troubleshooting; for
                        procurve-ssh (since 9.59) the ssh client's account of
                        the SSH authentication comes first, then the session
                        dump. The file is created with mode 0600 because it
                        contains the passwords as typed.

nexus-ssh
---------

The nexus-ssh module recognizes the following options: (See above
cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions.)

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
user                    Mandatory. Must land on the "#" prompt: NX-OS has no
                        "enable"; a ">" prompt is reported as an error.
pass                    Mandatory.
repository              Mandatory.
keep                    Mandatory.
timeout                 Optional, default 30 seconds (see the cisco-ios/
                        cisco-cat section).
banner_timeout          Optional.
prompt_settle_ms        Optional. Milliseconds to wait for output to go idle
                        before matching the command prompt (default 150; 0
                        disables).
fetch_timeout           Optional.
changes_only            Optional. The "!Time:" header line of the running-config
                        is the moment of the export and changes on every run; it
                        is kept in the backup but ignored when comparing, so it
                        alone never counts as a change. "!Running configuration
                        last done at:" changes only when the configuration
                        changed and does count.
on_fetch_run            Optional.
on_fetch_cat            Optional.
timezone                Optional.
filename_append_suffix  Optional.
show_cmd                Optional. If specified, its value will replace the usual
                        "show running-config" command. The pager is disabled
                        with "terminal length 0" beforehand.
debug                   Optional. Boolean. If specified as debug=on (default
                        off), records to <repository>/<dev_id>.debug (mode 0600)
                        first the ssh client's account of the SSH authentication
                        and then the raw switch session.

mediant-sbc
-----------

The mediant-sbc module recognizes the following options: (See above
cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions.)

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
user                    Mandatory. Login name (SSH or telnet).
pass                    Mandatory. Login password.
enable                  Mandatory. The "enable" password: the CLI lands on the
                        unprivileged "hostname> " prompt and the config is read
                        from the privileged "hostname# " prompt. A wrong enable
                        password is reported as such.
transport               Optional. "ssh" (default), "telnet" or "auto". "auto"
                        tries SSH first and falls back to telnet ONLY if no SSH
                        service answers (connection refused, unreachable, timed
                        out). It never falls back on an authentication,
                        key-exchange or host-key failure: that would retry the
                        password over a second channel (lockouts, alarms) and
                        move a credential from an encrypted to a cleartext
                        channel. A fallback is logged at info level so a
                        downgrade is always visible. On native Windows the SSH
                        path is unavailable, so use "telnet" (see Platform
                        support).
repository              Mandatory.
keep                    Mandatory.
timeout                 Optional, default 30 seconds (see the cisco-ios/
                        cisco-cat section).
banner_timeout          Optional.
prompt_settle_ms        Optional (SSH). Milliseconds to wait for output to go
                        idle before matching the prompt (default 150).
fetch_timeout           Optional. The full configuration is long; raise this on
                        slow links.
changes_only            Optional.
on_fetch_run            Optional.
on_fetch_cat            Optional.
timezone                Optional.
filename_append_suffix  Optional.
show_cmd                Optional. Replaces "show running-config" (settings that
                        differ from the firmware defaults - the form the
                        device's own CLI-script export produces and a restore
                        via "copy cli-script from ..." is designed for). "show
                        running-config full" lists every parameter including
                        defaults: better for auditing default changes across
                        firmware upgrades, riskier to replay on a newer
                        firmware. For both, add a second device entry with its
                        own dev_id, e.g. mediant-sbc SBC10 sbc10.example.com
                        mediant-sbc SBC10-full sbc10.example.com show_cmd="show
                        running-config full" The CLI pauses with " --MORE--"
                        after each screen. The pager settings are under
                        "configure system" -> "cli-settings":
                        default-window-height is persistent (part of the running
                        configuration) and the per-session override
                        window-height exists only inside that configuration
                        mode. A backup tool must not modify the configuration
                        nor enter configuration mode, so the pager is driven: a
                        space is sent at every marker and the marker and its
                        erase sequence are removed from the saved configuration.
pager_cmd               Optional. A command to send before the show command to
                        disable paging for the session, for a firmware that
                        offers one. Its rejection ("Invalid command", "%", ...)
                        aborts the fetch. Not needed on the observed Mediant
                        500Li.
debug                   Optional. Boolean. debug=on records to
                        <repository>/<dev_id>.debug (mode 0600); over SSH the
                        ssh client's debug trace comes first, then the raw
                        session.

procurve-snmp
-------------

The procurve-snmp module recognizes the following options: (See above
cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions of the common ones.)

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
community               Mandatory. The SNMP read-WRITE community. There is no
                        user/pass for this model; the write access is what arms
                        the TFTP export. Masked in logs.
repository              Mandatory.
keep                    Mandatory.
timeout                 Optional. Seconds, default 30. Applied to both the SNMP
                        session and the TFTP transfer.
snmp_version            Optional. Default snmpv2c.
oid                     Optional. The "copy/TFTP mode" trigger OID, set to 2 to
                        enable the export and 1 to disable it. Default
                        1.3.6.1.4.1.11.2.14.11.5.1.7.1.5.6.0 (as used by the
                        observed chassis); override only if a model uses a
                        different sub-OID.
remote_file             Optional. The TFTP filename to fetch, default "browse".
                        See ProCurve SNMP backups.
changes_only            Optional.
on_fetch_run            Optional.
on_fetch_cat            Optional.
timezone                Optional.
filename_append_suffix  Optional.
debug                   Optional. Boolean. If specified as debug=on (default
                        off), records the SNMP sets and the TFTP transfer to
                        <repository>/<dev_id>.debug.

tplink-web-sg105e, procurve-web-1700
------------------------------------

The tplink-web-sg105e and procurve-web-1700 modules recognize the following
options: (See above cisco-ios/cisco-cat section for descriptions.)

Option                  Description
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
user                    Mandatory for tplink-web-sg105e: the web UI login name.
                        Ignored by procurve-web-1700, whose web UI has only a
                        manager password.
pass                    Mandatory. The web UI password.
repository              Mandatory.
keep                    Mandatory.
timeout                 Mandatory. Applies to each HTTP request.
fetch_timeout           Optional. Applies to the config download request only;
                        the web UI itself warns that the backup can take a
                        while.
changes_only            Optional.
on_fetch_run            Optional.
on_fetch_cat            Optional.
timezone                Optional.
filename_append_suffix  Optional.
debug                   Optional. Boolean. If specified as debug=on (default
                        off), records every HTTP request and response of the run
                        (password masked, binary bodies summarized by size) to
                        <repository>/<dev_id>.debug for troubleshooting.

show_cmd (most CLI models)
--------------------------

If specified, replaces the model's usual show-config command  -  useful for
older devices. The default is the model's native command (e.g. show run on
cisco-ios/cisco-cat, show running-config on most others, show running-config all
on hirschmann). On cisco-ios, show_cmd=wrterm is kept as a backward-compatible
alias for write term in place of show run.

debug
-----

Boolean, available for all models (since v9.71). If debug=on (default off),
records the raw telnet/SSH session (or, for the web UI models, every HTTP
request and response; for procurve-snmp, the SNMP operations and TFTP transfer)
to <repository>/<dev_id>.debug (mode 0600, login/enable passwords kept out) for
troubleshooting. Overwritten on each run. Enable only while troubleshooting and
delete the file afterwards, as it can still contain configuration secrets.

Troubleshooting
===============

    WARNING: Legacy SSH key exchange
    Some older SSH implementations (e.g. older HP/Aruba ProCurve firmware) only
    offer legacy key exchange algorithms that modern OpenSSH clients disable by
    default. fetchconfig.pl itself already re-enables what it needs for its own
    SSH connections (see the KexAlgorithms/HostKeyAlgorithms/Ciphers master_opts
    in ProCurveSSH.pm and ComwareSSH.pm), so this only matters if you want to
    connect to the same device manually with the system's own ssh command  -
    the same kind of algorithm may need to be re-enabled in the SSH client's own
    configuration:

    `/etc/ssh_config
    Host *
        KexAlgorithms +diffie-hellman-group1-sha1
    `

Security notes
==============

See also the Directory allowlist, which restricts the directories a device table
may reference  -  important when the table is edited through a less-trusted
front end.

    WARNING: Debug files contain credentials
    With debug=on, <repository>/<dev_id>.debug records the session verbatim  -
    for the telnet/SSH models that includes the login and enable passwords
    exactly as typed, and for procurve-snmp the SNMP read-write community. For
    the SSH models the file begins with the ssh client's debug trace (the same
    output as ssh -v: key exchange, host key, authentication methods tried); ssh
    never writes the password itself into that trace. Since v9.54 these files
    are created with mode 0600 regardless of the process umask. On native
    Windows these mode bits are a no-op (NTFS uses ACLs); confidentiality relies
    on the containing folder's ACLs  -  see Platform support. Still, enable
    debug=on only while troubleshooting and delete the file afterwards. Backup
    files, their directories and the .status file are created with the process
    umask  -  an unprivileged reader such as fetchconfig-web (running as nobody
    for -l/-g) must be able to read them, so they are deliberately not forced
    owner-only (parallel-fetch worker scratch files, in a private temp dir, are
    owner-only). Backups contain configuration secrets  -  and not always
    hashed: SNMP communities and some routing/VPN keys (BGP, OSPF,
    RADIUS/TACACS, IPsec pre-shared keys) are cleartext, and Cisco "type 7"
    passwords are reversible. Restrict the repository directory on a shared host
    and treat every backup as sensitive.

SSH host keys are not verified. The SSH models use StrictHostKeyChecking=no and
UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null: any host key is accepted and none remembered. This
is deliberate  -  fetchconfig runs unattended, and a switch's host key changes
on every hardware swap, which would otherwise halt backups until someone edited
known_hosts. The trade-off is that an on-path attacker could impersonate a
device and capture the login password. Run fetchconfig on a trusted management
network.

Device IDs are validated. A dev_id may contain letters, digits, underscore, dot
and dash (hostnames like sw01.example.com are fine) but may not consist only of
dots, since . or .. would resolve to the repository itself or its parent.

SMTP. AUTH LOGIN sends the password base64-encoded, not encrypted  -  see the
tls option under E-mail notifications. Header fields (From, To, Subject) have
line breaks stripped before sending, so a device-table value cannot inject extra
mail headers.

Non-ASCII hostnames. aruba-cx-ssh and planet-ssh handle a prompt containing
spaces or non-ASCII characters. procurve, procurve-ssh, hirschmann, comware-ssh
and nec-univerge-ix still build their prompt regexp with quotemeta() and would
not match such a prompt; this is left unchanged pending tests against real
devices  -  keep hostnames on those devices to ASCII.

Status file
===========

After every backup attempt fetchconfig writes a small status file next to the
backups, at <repository>/<dev_id>.status. It is written for every device and
every model, and does not depend on the email: line  -  it is a plain repository
artifact, meant for external tools (for example a web front-end that reads
backup_duration_sec to size a progress spinner). It is overwritten on every run
and written atomically, so a reader never sees a half-written file.

    [swi-lab-01]
    backup_start_date=2026-09-11
    backup_start_time=22:14:03
    backup_end_date=2026-09-11
    backup_end_time=22:14:09
    backup_duration_sec=6
    backup_state=success
    backup_changed=changed

Key                                    Value
-------------------------------------  -----------------------------------------
backup_start_date / backup_start_time  When the fetch started (local time).
                                       Derived as end minus backup_duration_sec.
backup_end_date / backup_end_time      When the fetch finished (local time).
backup_duration_sec                    Whole seconds the fetch took - the same
                                       value logged for the run and shown in the
                                       summary e-mail.
backup_state                           success, failed, or skipped.
backup_changed                         initial for a device's first-ever backup
                                       (no previous config to compare, whatever
                                       changes_only is); then unchanged or
                                       changed for a changes_only=1 device;
                                       forced for a changes_only=0 device (saved
                                       regardless of any comparison); unknown
                                       when the state is failed or skipped.

Backup file naming
==================

Backups are named <dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS[<tz>][<suffix>], i.e. with
one-second resolution, where <suffix> is the optional filename_append_suffix
(must start with a "."; see the Options reference). They are stored in a
three-level dated tree under the repository:
<repository>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<dev_id>/.

<tz> is whatever strftime produced on the backup host and so differs by platform
and changes with daylight saving time: a numeric offset (+0200, -0500) on Linux
and modern Windows; a bare name (CEST in summer, CET in winter; NFT/DFT on older
AIX) on AIX; -CEST on Solaris; a multi-word name such as W. Europe Standard Time
(or a localized one) on older Windows; or nothing with timezone=hide. Since
v9.55 every operation that orders backups ("latest" for changes_only, keep=N
expiry, -g/-n/-m, -l, -z/-Z, -s/-S) orders them by the parsed timestamp
YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS rather than by a plain string sort of the whole name, so neither
the tz token nor a suffix can disturb the order.

Two backups of the same device completed within the same second would get the
same name. Rather than let the second silently overwrite the first (and then
compare the file with itself), the backup is written with O_EXCL: the second run
fails to create the file and is reported, leaving the first intact. This
essentially never happens for a real fetch (login, pager, full configuration and
logout take well over a second, and scheduled runs are minutes apart), and
device locking (lock=on) prevents concurrent same-device runs outright; it only
arises in artificial back-to-back test runs.

